543	{"artifactId":"LLD-fd8d70c3e8c397ea-s1","entry":{"id":"a07eaf54a1204f70b454a19b51e29c65","actor":{"id":"codex:shutdown-design","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:12:18.585Z","operation":"update","revision":{"number":5,"contentHash":"ddcffc26f94487395ac2ad05c2ffe5eaf4b8fed089e2a488e316931e2900758b"},"outcome":{"result":"revised","summary":"Address design-review HIGH: include daemon-side PID/socket ownership, startup publication and async request-server close in the run-file ownership protocol."},"addresses":"all"}}
7090	{"artifactId":"LLD-fd8d70c3e8c397ea-s1","entry":{"id":"review-3f99d05b6b82362dabdaaa5c5f715c97","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:12:33.599Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"fix-targets-defect.1","ref":"fix-targets-defect","item":"fix-targets-defect","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Retaining the original process identity and adding independent termination protection address the two operational faults stated in the ISSUE.","evidence":"ISSUE.md identifies file-based stop success and a process remaining alive after 'bye'. waitForStop returns true when both paths disappear; stopDaemonWith immediately returns stopped:true on that result. createShutdown clears run files and logs 'bye' before exitRespectingStoreHealth. Its backstop runs on the same event loop. The proposed retained target and independent helper directly address these mechanisms. Historical diagnosis is explicitly deferred by the stated stakeholder decision. [files: docs/standalone/daemon-stop-must-wait-process-end-E20261013fd8d70c3/ISSUE.md, src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts, src/daemon/shutdown.ts]","action":"Implement both protections and retain the explicit historical-diagnosis caveat.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Both CLI and script currently infer successful stop from disappearing run files and can lose the original process target.","evidence":"CLI waitForStop checks '!existsSync(PATHS.pidFile) && !existsSync(PATHS.sockFile)' and returns true. stopDaemonWith obtains its forced-stop PID through livePid() only after waiting. The script similarly returns success for absent PID/socket and force_stop_daemon rereads PID_FILE. [files: src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts, scripts/daemon-ctl.sh]","action":"Capture identity before RPC or SIGTERM and use it throughout waiting and forced termination.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design identifies the production paths that publish or explicitly remove managed PID/socket files and the shutdown wiring that must change.","evidence":"daemon/lifecycle.ts contains writePid, clearPid and stale cleanup through isAlreadyRunning. IpcServer.listen removes the socket before binding; close awaits server.close then removes it again. index.ts calls isAlreadyRunning, writePid and server.listen, and starts 'void server.close()' from stopBackgroundWork. CLI lifecycle, daemon service and control script also remove run files. All these production files appear in the design's change-site lists. [files: src/daemon/lifecycle.ts, src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/index.ts, src/daemon/shutdown.ts, src/daemon/fatal.ts, src/cli/services/lifecycle.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts, scripts/daemon-ctl.sh]","action":"Apply the ownership contract to every identified path, including realEndDeps used by fatal handling; await guarded server-close completion rather than retaining the current fire-and-forget call.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"preserved-behaviour.1","ref":"preserved-behaviour","item":"preserved-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Store-health-aware termination, reentrancy protection and restart ordering are existing behaviors that the design preserves.","evidence":"createShutdown returns immediately on a repeated signal. Its locks-lost branch skips queue waiting and closeStores, and endAfterStoreFailure uses killSelf rather than process.exit. maintenance.restart awaits stopDaemon and returns from its catch before startDaemon. Existing shutdown-store-failure tests assert skipped close, SIGKILL and healthy exit; daemon-stop tests assert no start after stop failure. [files: src/daemon/shutdown.ts, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts, src/daemon/__tests__/shutdown-store-failure.test.ts, src/cli/services/__tests__/daemon-stop.test.ts]","action":"Keep these branches and ordering while adding watchdog readiness and ownership handling.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"preserved-behaviour.2","ref":"preserved-behaviour","item":"preserved-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The shared run-file guard can remain fail-closed after owner death while allowing the designed forced-stop and subsequent restart to succeed.","evidence":"The design requires holding the guard across asynchronous server.close and refuses reclamation after its owner exits. In current wiring, server.close completion can depend on connected clients, while disconnectClients runs later in closeStores. The design's watchdog or CLI SIGKILL can therefore terminate a daemon while it holds this guard. A subsequent startup then encounters exactly the orphaned guard the design requires it to reject, so successful forced termination need not permit restart. Existing maintenance.restart unconditionally proceeds to startDaemon after a successful stop. [files: src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/index.ts, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts, src/cli/services/daemon.ts]","action":"Define a safe recovery protocol for a guard whose owner is positively verified dead, or explicitly propagate an unrecovered ownership conflict as stop/restart failure. Add a test that kills the guard owner during asynchronous close and attempts restart.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The proposed test strategy maps each acceptance criterion to a check capable of detecting its corresponding missing fix.","evidence":"ac1 proposes keeping the original process alive after deleting run files, directly exposing today's false success. ac2 proposes a blocked exit hook and healthy control, distinguishing independent protection from the current event-loop timer. ac3 proposes reused identity, replacement files and failed-stop restart checks. ac4 specifies manual inspection of reproduction evidence and the historical-cause caveat. Existing forced-stop fixtures leave run files present while stubborn processes live, so those tests alone do not cover ac1. [files: src/cli/services/__tests__/daemon-stop.test.ts, src/cli/services/__tests__/daemon-stop-forced.test.ts, src/cli/services/__tests__/daemon-ctl-forced-stop.test.ts, src/daemon/__tests__/shutdown-store-failure.test.ts]","action":"Implement the proposed regression cases; treat ac4 as manual acceptance and include the guard-owner-death restart case.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":0,"low":5,"unverified":0},"template":"design-issue","reviewedAt":"2026-10-24T15:12:33.599Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
